   "id": "c_lamps__s1",
   "chapter": "c_lamps",
   "scene": 0,
   "text": "Rain came to Vesk the way gossip did: sideways, all at once, and from the sea. By the time it reached the guild steps Kael had lit sixty lamps, and his matches had gone soft as bread.\n\nMira was waiting under the awning with the ledger open on her knee. She did not look up. She never looked up for anything less than a dark lamp, and there were no dark lamps yet.\n\n“Sixty,” Kael said.\n\n“Sixty-one,” she said, and pointed with her chin at the lamp above the bakery door, which he had missed. Then she reached into her coat and handed him the heel of the loaf, the way she always did, still warm, wrapped in a page torn from last year’s ledger.\n\nHe ate it on the way back down. Four hundred and twelve stairs to the harbor, and he counted every one, because counting was what he did instead of being afraid, and tonight, for no reason he could name, he was afraid.",
